  "subtitle": "professional-grade light pen for the Apple II, by the teenager who had worked at Stanford AI Lab at age 15",
  "why_scout_cares": "The most technically sophisticated light pen system for any 8-bit microcomputer, created by Steve Gibson — the same Steve Gibson who later founded Gibson Research Corporation, wrote SpinRite, and co-hosts Security Now!. Gibson's career arc is extraordinary: hired by Stanford AI Lab at age 15, built the LPS II at 26, sold the company to Atari, recovered the rights, licensed to Koala Technologies, and later became one of the longest-tenured independent software developers. The LPS II offered 1:1 absolute positioning on the Apple II's CRT — direct-manipulation drawing with a precision that reviewers described as 'utterly analogous to working with a normal pen.' At $250 it sat between the KoalaPad ($125) and professional CAD tablets ($1,095+), bringing professional-grade light pen interaction to home computers. A specimen is held by the Computer History Museum. No light pen exists in the current collection.",
